<table cellpadding="3" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%" align="center" class="tborder" style="border-bottom: 0;">
	<tr class="titlebg">
		<td valign="middle" align="left" width="15%" style="padding-left: 6px;">
			<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/topic/normal_post.gif" alt="" />
			Author
		</td>
		<td valign="middle" align="left" width="85%" style="padding-left: 6px;">
			Topic: Chapter 5 &nbsp;(Read 120 times)
		</td>
	</tr>
</table>
<table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%" class="bordercolor">
	<tr><td style="padding: 1px 1px 0 1px;">
		<table cellpadding="3" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%">
			<tr><td class="windowbg">
				<table width="100%" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
					<tr>
						<td valign="top" width="15%" rowspan="2">
							<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?action=profile;u=81" title="View the profile of Phoebie">Phoebie</a></b><br />
							<span class="smalltext">
								Orc Pawn<br />
								<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/star.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><br />
								Posts: 15<br />
								<br />
								<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/YaBBImages/avatars/blank.gif" alt="" border="0" /><br />
								I'm a llama!<br />
								<br />
								
								
								
								<br />
								<a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?action=profile;u=81"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/icons/profile_sm.gif" alt="View Profile" title="View Profile" border="0" /></a>
								<a href="mailto:brneydmzo@bellsouth.net"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/email_sm.gif" alt="Email" title="Email" border="0" /></a>
							</span>
						</td>
						<td valign="top" width="85%" height="100%">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=119.msg374#msg374"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=119.msg374#msg374">Chapter 5</a></b><br />
									<span class="smalltext">&#171; <b> on:</b> April 10, 2003, 02:03:07 pm &#187;</span></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							Keeping herself invisible and close to the towering black walls, the petite wood elf shivered briefly as the howling screams of the trapped spirits in this place cried out in the dark tongue, cursing the name of her goddess. &nbsp;Speeding her pace, she reached up behind her head and quickly twisted her long red hair into a knot, securing it with a bit of string she pulled from her backpack. &nbsp;The air here was clammy and reeked of decay, and she could already begin to feel the beads of perspiration popping up along her neckline and upper lip. &nbsp;<br /><br />Edaar spotted her as she approached, the unmistakable sway of her hips as she walked towards him something that always caught the eye of men wherever she went. &nbsp;Edaar grinned widely, and hollered out her name. Phoebie recognized the blonde hair and boyish face of her ageless mage friend, and running towards him, she embraced him warmly, noticing that he was thinner than usual, his face a bit more gaunt and drawn than it was the last time she had seen him. &nbsp;She made a mental note to question him about his health later, and pulled back, her hands upon his face.<br /><br />&quot;My sweet friend, I have missed you much,&quot; she whispered to him. &nbsp;Her voice was lyrical, the elven language she spoke slightly accented, reminding Edaar of the giggling voices of wood elves he would hear in the forests of the Greater Faydark when he was a boy. &nbsp;He had always been told that the wood elves were the darker, wilder more unpredictable relatives of his own high elven race, but he found the wood elves playful, earthy and full of magic granted to them by the forests, magic he was not kin to, magic that allowed them to harness the winds and the rains and the earth itself, magic that charmed animals, sensed danger, and showed them footsteps of enemies that may have been missed by a common observer. &nbsp;The eyes of the wood elves saw things dark and wonderful hidden in a breeze, or a snowflake, or the falling leaves of autumn; their powers of observation were overwhelming.<br /><br />The smile that stretched across her face as she looked deeply at him warmed him to the very core. &quot;My lady Phoebie, even a place as ugly as this can't help but be blessed by your beauty and grace. We are pleased you have arrived safely.&quot;<br /><br />As Edaar returned quietly to his meditaions, Phoebie slowly glanced around with large green eyes, surveying the faces of her friends and comrades, her heart's pace slowing and relaxing now that she was once again among the people she loved most in the world.<br /><br />Hearing the rumbling laughter of a dwarf emitting from around the corner, she instantly recognized the chuckle as that of her dear friend Spudds, a dwarven cleric from the halls of Kaladim, one whom she had met in her younger days when she would venture to the Butcherblock Mountains in search of wild fruits and vegtables for her mother's stews and pies. &nbsp;<br /><br />She had met him as she waited nervously one afternoon at the crossroads, trying to get directions from a rather rude and gruff guard who sat at his post, beer in hand, complaining about the &quot;damn &nbsp;(HIC!) goblins that were taking over the (HICCUP) blasted countryside&quot;. &nbsp;Just when she had given up hope of gleaning any useful information from the impossibly disgruntled and intoxicated soldier, she had felt a little hand tugging at the hem of her tunic, and had looked down into the warm brown laughing eyes of a dwarf not much older than her, his beard already down past his chin, his sweaty hand wrapped firmly around a hammer that was coated with the guts of some unfortunate creature he had just recently pummeled.<br />&quot;This way, my little strawberry tart, I'll show ye to the valley where ye can find those thar goodies, but only if ya promise to give me a kiss when we arrive! Harrharrr!&quot; &nbsp;He took off quickly down the rocky path, his stumpy legs surprisingly fast, and she had followed him hesitantly at first, not sure she wanted her lips anywhere near the scruffy face of the sweaty dorf, but nonetheless willing to follow him instead of wander lost for another hour through these strange mountains. &nbsp;Spudds was a gentleman at heart, and after they arrived at their destination, she had reached down to shake his hand and thank him sincerely, overly professional, and she saw his countenance fall as he realized he would not get his kiss. &nbsp;&quot;Well, girl, I will get my kiss someday, I know it...for now, happy foraging and remember, if ye are ever in need of a cleric, I live over in the third hut from the left outside the Kaladim gates, just come a knocking. I live alone right now, so you can show up anytime!&quot; He wiggled his eyebrows suggestively, and with a loud hollering laugh, he took off back down the path, panting and huffing as his little legs carried him efficiently across the rocky terrain.<br /><br />She saw Spudds sitting now, drinking an ale, flipping through a book who's title she could barely make out in the darkness. &quot;The Bearded Women of Kaladim: What Makes Us So Darn Sexy?&quot; seemed to be the name, and she giggled as she watched Spudds jostle his beer all down the front of his tunic as he encountered a particularly funny passage in the text.<br /><br />Next to Spudds sat Uuldiin, a new recruit to Anthology, a wood elf like herself, but one with dark brooding eyes and a wicked smile that told her he was always up to no good. &nbsp;He grinned at her unkowingly at first, then recognizing her as a fellow druid, he raised his hand in salute, nodding slightly and respectfully to her, his eyes wandering over her appreciatively before returning to the tailoring patterns he was studying.<br /><br />Depraven, a necromancer of legendary powers and one of the newest members of the guild, was stretched out in a corner, pretending to be dead, as usual, but upon seeing Phoebie he stood up, stretched his arms above his head, and yawned. &nbsp;&quot;Hello Phoebie,&quot; he crooned, the low timbre of his voice rattling in his throat. &quot;I have not seen you since last month in the Temple of Snakes. How have you been?&quot; &nbsp;Phoebie nodded at him, her hands reaching out to straighten an errant white hair that hung salaciously over his left eyebrow. &nbsp;He was surprised at her gesture of affection, but he did not shrink from her touch. &nbsp;&quot;Aaaah, thank you m'lady, I have hair as wild as the Karanas, I fear it will never do as it should.&quot; &nbsp;He grinned at her, his teeth white and straight, his eyes a deep purple color, rimmed with snow colored eyelashes and eyebrows, the small goatee on his chin of the same bright white. &quot;Perhaps if you did not spend so much time lying down, Depraven, your hair would be less likely to fall out of place.&quot; &nbsp;He chuckled, and returned to his nap, his mouth spreading in a wide yawn as he found a soft spot of ground once again and dozed off into sleep.<br /><br />The strains of singing could be heard from beneath a nearby tent, and recognizing the lilting libretto of her friend Garlick, Phoebie walked over to the hastily erected tent, peering her head in to see the form of her friend huddled over a score of music that seemed impossibly complicated. &quot;laaaa, lari larooo, marilar, moroona larayna...Dammit, that is not right.&quot; &nbsp;The stunning half-elven woman rolled her eyes and sighed heavily, she was apparently trying to learn a new song, and was having some trouble with it. Garlick looked up briefly, her eyes catching sight of Phoebie peering through the tent flap, and she stood up a bit too quickly, hitting her head on the tentpole. Swearing softly, she rubbed her skull gingerly, her dark auburn hair standing curly and wild atop her head. &nbsp;No amount of pomade could seem to tame her red hair, which grew in every which direction, curling around her ears and across her forehead, giving her the impression of a feisty wild fairy, her dimpled face and sparkling amber colored eyes adding to her ageless appearance. &nbsp;&quot;Phoebs, what the heck? I didn't know you were coming up here!&quot; Phoebie grinned, embraced her friend tenderly and spoke. &quot;Yes, I heard the news about Cinamini. Zzapp, Voltis, Keshanti, Kaidog, Kaiyne and I raced here when we heard what had hapened...&quot; Phoebie paused, contemplating. &quot;Aussie must be devastated.&quot; &nbsp;Garlick nodded somberly, her normally rosy cheeks drained of their color. &quot;We are trying to comfort him, but his grief is hard to assauge.&quot; &nbsp;Phoebie nodded knowingly at her friend, squeezed her shoulder gently, and kept walking.<br /><br />She was looking for Karadana, she had to find her and tell her of the new warrior she had recruited while passing through Halas a few weeks earlier. &nbsp;Eager for adventure, the barbarian had nearly knocked her over that frosty morning as she walked to the kennels in Halas to purchase a sled dog for a journey she had to make to Everfrost. &nbsp;He had noticed how out of place the freckled redheaded elven girl was amidst the amazon-sized barbarians that trudged through the village. &nbsp;He had followed her as she wandered from shop to shop, browsing the wares and weavings of the vendors, and he was caught off guard as she stopped in her tracks quickly, turned to face him with a fire burning in her green eyes, and asked him loudly. &quot;WHAT are you doing, warrior? Did you think I wouldn't notice you following me? What do you want?&quot; &nbsp;He instantly noticed the crest of Anthology on her armor, and realized she was part of one of the most powerful guilds in Norrath. &nbsp;He also had heard rumors that Karadana had joined the ranks of Anthology, and he instantly formulated a plan in his head, one that, if successful, would take him from these frosty peaks and perhaps back into the arms of the woman he had lost so very long ago to the lure of adventure. &nbsp;Kaidog chuckled, backed away from her slowly, his hands raised in submission. &quot;Take it easy there, pointy ear. I just wanted to ask you about your guild, and see if you would be needin the services of a brave warlord such as myself...&quot;<br /><br />Their conversation had progressed smoothly over a cup of spiced cider in the local pub, his treat of course, and in a matter of hours he had packed his bags and followed the feisty wood elf south to the Deserts of Ro where they had met up with a wizened old Erudite wizard named Zzapp, a catperson named Keshanti, another barbarian shaman named Voltis and a half-elven paladin named Kaiyne. Before he knew it, he had been transported to this dark morbid plane they called Hate. &nbsp;He didn't worry now about the monsters that lurked nearby, he was more worried about seeing Karadana again, after so many years. &nbsp;Would she shun him? Would she even remember him? What words would she have for him, having left him so many years ago just as their romance had begun to blossom? &nbsp;Questions assaulted his brain with rapid succession. &nbsp;He began to sweat uncontrollably, his hands clammy, his mouth as dry as cotton. &nbsp;He stepped down off of the stone platform and followed Kaiyne towards the camp, wondering what her first words would be to him after so many years of silence.
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" colspan="2" class="smalltext">
								</td>
							</tr><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
								</td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
									<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/ip.gif" alt="" border="0" /> Logged
								</td>
							</tr></table>
						</td>
					</tr>
				</table>
			</td></tr>
		</table>
	</td></tr>
</table>
